  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/8b/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_50.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Young bully Dave Cannon was born with the ability to \"whirl around at great speed.\"&nbsp; He uses his abilities throughout his young years to make money, and finally sets out in the present day as the \"Human Top.\"&nbsp; Hank Pym sees a newspaper article about him and calls the Wasp to join him to try and track him down.\n\nThey get a tip from the ants about him planning to rob Danly's Department Store the next morning at 10 AM.&nbsp; Posing as an ice skater, Cannon finally dons his new Human Top disguise to hide his identity when he sets out to rob the department store's payroll.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And we finally see the first traces of what will become the more familiar Whirlwind costume.]\n\nEven though Hank and Jan are both present at the store when he steals the cash, he's too fast for them, eventually escaping through the subway, with Giant-Man lumbering along too slowly to effectively follow him.&nbsp; The Human Top is simply too nimble and is <i>easily</i> able to slip past Hank's every attempt to grab him.\n\nHank realizes the design flaw of Giant-Man and works to increase his speed and reflexes, even training against a spinning \"gyro-top\" operated by Jan.&nbsp; But she can immediately see that he can't handle the mechanism and has <i>no hope</i> of catching the Top.\n\nMeanwhile, the Top decides that Giant-Man and Wasp are too dangerous for him to ignore.&nbsp; He knows he'll eventually slip up and be captured, so he decides to attack first, and in the last panel, we see him walking down a street with a bundle of dynamite that he's already lit!\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "A modest story elevated by a sharp chase and a cliff‑hanger that hints at bigger things to come.&nbsp; Flawed, <i>yes</i> -- but it leaves a mark that goes beyond its pages.",
  "subtitle": "When the Villain Has a Plan... and the Hero Has a Problem",
  "review_text": "The story as a whole wasn't particularly noteworthy, but I did at least like the villain and the way he exposed the inherent weakness of a large, lumbering hero like Giant-Man.\n\nIt was also interesting that we as readers were told that Dave Cannon was <i>born</i> with the ability to twirl around, so much so that he even became <i>well-known</i> for it.&nbsp; (But Stan stops short of ever referring to him as a \"mutant,\" which I imagine he could have gotten away with here.)&nbsp; I <b>do</b> question how he managed to conceal his identity in the present, given that he never made any attempt to hide it as a <i>child</i>.\n\n[ASSET: left | HumanTopAtLarge | width=22%] [ASSET: right | HumanTop | width=16%] As these panels show, <i>years</i> went by without the police ever learning who the Human Top was.&nbsp; (You'd think that any police detective worth his salt would be able to deduce that a youngster with a well-known spinning ability (who had been previously caught stealing fruit, as page 2 <i>clearly</i> shows) <i>might</i> be a prime suspect for the mysterious, so-called \"spinning criminal\" ultimately given the name \"Human Top\".\n\nIt's a <i>baffling</i> oversight, especially given how small the pool of known human whirlwinds must have been.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | WingAtFullSize | width=7%] [ASSET: right | WingsGoneAgain | width=12%] On various pages (particularly in the beginning), the Wasp is shown with wings visible despite being full-size.&nbsp; Even on those pages, it seems to go back and forth between wings and no wings.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's not a stylistic choice or a story beat -- it's simply inconsistent [SPAN: severity=error; text=and a clear mistake.]\n\n[ASSET: left | StrictlyBusiness | width=27%] [ASSET: right | Honey | width=14%] The pages are filled with the Wasp's never-ending flirting, expressing her disappointment that Hank hasn't summoned her for any reason other than business.&nbsp; But you can't blame her for getting mixed signals, especially when Hank calls her \"honey.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It seems he can't make up his mind whether Jan is a \"kid\" or his \"honey.\")]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPage 5 reveals (I <i>think</i> for the first time) how Giant-Man actually communicates with the ants.&nbsp; Here, we see that the ants don't actually <i>speak</i>.&nbsp; Instead, their limited minds are only able to send images that Hank must interpret.\n[ASSET: center | ThoughtWaveCommunicator | width=67%]\nYou <i>have</i> to admit these images are <i>amazingly</i> specific.&nbsp; A <i>named</i> department store?&nbsp; August 4 at 10 AM?&nbsp; <b><i>Seriously</i></b><i>?</i>\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But, then again, it <i>is</i> a comic book!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | EvadingCapture | width=19%] Pages 8-10 depict Giant-Man and the Wasp's first encounter with the Human Top, and were [SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent], easily demonstrating how a swiftly-moving foe could dart around traffic and down subway tunnels to avoid the awkward, lumbering shape of Giant-Man, while staying one step ahead of the Wasp.\n[BR]\n[BR] Cannon seemed to realize this as well, as he continued to taunt the far slower Giant-Man, who, whether he liked it or not, got a masterclass on how to evade capture!\n\n[ASSET: right | WontWork | width=11%] Giant-Man realizes that his entire power‑set is fundamentally ill‑suited to this opponent.&nbsp; His only choice is to retreat and try to enhance his abilities, even going so far as to develop a serum to increase his speed and engage in training against a whirling \"gyro‑top.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] But as this panel shows, even the Wasp recognizes that, against a speedy opponent like the Human Top, Giant-Man is simply out of his league.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nTo Cannon's credit, even after he successfully gets away, he acknowledges that his criminal career will [SPAN: severity=warning; text=never truly be safe] as long as Giant-Man and the Wasp are out there, looking for him.&nbsp; It's a surprisingly forward-thinking realization for a villain who otherwise comes across as a disposable and one-off opponent for the title's heroes.\n\n[ASSET: left | ToBeContinued | width=18%] Still.. you <i>do </i>have to question his choice to light the fuse on the dynamite bundle\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=before reaching his destination!]\n[BR]\n[BR] I think you have to agree:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=that does not seem very smart!]\n[BR] That's not <b>bold</b>; it's <i>wreckless</i> in a way only Cannon could justify to himself.\n[BR] Why not wait to get where you're going <i>before</i> lighting the fuse?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Come on, man!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nSteve Ditko is credited for the inking over Jack Kirby's pencils, but Kirby must have done such a thorough job that I saw no trace of any Ditko-ish figures.&nbsp; Many inkers leave a trace of their own style (Vinnie Colletta, Tony DeZuniga, Pablo Marcus, just to name three).\n\nGiven Ditko's normally-distinctive figure work, his near‑total invisibility here is striking.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe fact that the police never suspected Dave Cannon as the true identity of the mysterious Human Top did negatively affect the final grade, but as <i>Tales to Astonish</i> stories go, this one wasn't as poor as so many of them have been (so much so, that I actually [SPAN: severity=error; text=dread] the mornings when they're next up on the list).\n\nIt wasn't brilliant, but the way the Human Top kept slipping out of Giant-Man's reach time and again was fascinating to watch, and [SPAN: severity=info; text=I never got bored.]&nbsp; And other than the lit fuse at the end, the story lacked any [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"you gotta be <i>kidding</i> me\"] moments, which for this magazine especially, was a major <i>plus</i>.\n\nBut perhaps <b>most noteworthy</b> was one of the earliest [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=TO BE CONTINUED] endings.&nbsp; While the words themselves do not actually appear, and also, while we've certainly been left hanging at the end of an issue before (see [LINK:1961:FF_3|FF #3]'s ending with the Human Torch flying away), all the previous ones came <i>after</i> the main plot thread of the issue had been wrapped up.&nbsp; But in <i>this</i> instance, Giant-Man and the Wasp are still in the [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>middle</i> of the case], making the ending feel far more abrupt.\n\nAnyone trapped here with me in the 1960s (because of [PAGE: scenario]) has \"future memories\" that <i>have to</i> include the fact that continued endings are simply par for the course in comics, and yet, at this moment in time, an ending like this, which stops <i>everything</i> and tells the reader to [SPAN: severity=warning; text=tune back in again next month] -- this was still in its <b>infancy</b> at the time, and its abruptness would have felt genuinely new, so [SPAN: severity=success; text=kudos to Stan and Jack for taking such a bold step here]; counting on their audience to return for more in the next issue.\n\nSince I'm already on the subject of \"future memories,\" it's hard not to notice that this kind of cliff‑hanger energy will later become a staple of 1960s pop culture -- most famously in the <i>Batman</i> television series.&nbsp; Seeing that same spirit of \"[SPAN: severity=warning; text=tune in next time]\" forming right here in this issue from a good [SPAN: severity=info; text=three years before that show ever debuted], not only gives the issue an extra bit of charm, but it's a legacy worth celebrating with additional credit!\n\nEven <i>without</i> the cut-off ending, [SPAN: severity=info; text=the feeling of helplessness on the part of the heroes is real and gives the issue a somber tone it hasn't previously portrayed.]\n\nLet me be clear:&nbsp; this story will never rank near the top of Marvel greatness, but it did (as the splash page <i>literally</i> states) \"set a standard for years to come!\"&nbsp; And for once, the issue actually [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=earns that promise.]",
  "side_note": [
    "The splash page indicates that the main Giant-Man/Wasp tale is \"double-length,\" but by my count, it is only 13 pages, like normal.",
    "The backup tale \"The Secret of Sagattus!\" again spelled the word \"mannequin\" differently than the way you normally see it (see previous example in [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91]), except that they didn't even spell the alternate form \"manikin\" correctly -- here it was spelled \"maniken\"-- [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=oops!]"
  ],
  "highlight": "The Human Top stays a step ahead of Giant‑Man and the Wasp, right up to a <i>dynamite</i> cliffhanger ending."
}